1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,929
A storm is violent.

2
00:00:01,929 --> 00:00:03,908
Soft is gentle.

3
00:00:03,908 --> 00:00:09,351
Put them together, soft storm, and you get a contradiction, an oxymoron.

4
00:00:09,351 --> 00:00:14,839
That contradiction, in the title and repeated through the poem, is the whole argument.

5
00:00:15,067 --> 00:00:26,514
Abhi Subedi watches Kathmandu in a violent decade: hungry children, a lost child with a radio, a man beaten for no reason, a blood stained shirt.

6
00:00:26,514 --> 00:00:28,155
He does not become angry.

7
00:00:28,155 --> 00:00:29,561
He becomes soft.

8
00:00:29,561 --> 00:00:30,320
What does that mean?

9
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:42,126
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 12: Soft Storm, by the Nepali poet Abhi Subedi.

10
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:45,595
Six parts.

11
00:00:45,595 --> 00:00:47,926
Abhi Subedi and his Kathmandu.

12
00:00:47,926 --> 00:00:49,580
How the softness began.

13
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:51,650
Four scenes from a violent decade.

14
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:53,668
Crocuses over stone.

15
00:00:53,668 --> 00:00:54,840
The themes.

16
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,283
And the form and devices.

17
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:13,148
Abhi Subedi was born in nineteen forty five in Terhathum, in the eastern hills, and taught English at Tribhuvan University for about forty years; many of Nepal's English teachers were his students.

18
00:01:13,148 --> 00:01:19,489
He is a poet, playwright, critic and translator, and he writes in both Nepali and English.

19
00:01:19,489 --> 00:01:28,434
The textbook gives no date for this poem, but its violent history and war memories point clearly to Nepal's conflict years.

20
00:01:28,434 --> 00:01:31,650
Say that, rather than naming a book you cannot verify.

21
00:01:32,867 --> 00:01:36,866
Only two place names appear, a few hundred metres apart.

22
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:41,866
Thamel is the tourist quarter, Kathmandu's brightest and richest street.

23
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:50,056
Kesharmahal is a Rana palace with the Kaiser Library and the Garden of Dreams, and old trees where fruit bats really roost.

24
00:01:50,056 --> 00:01:58,676
So homeless children cry with hunger under the bat bearing trees of a palace full of books and a garden built for pleasure.

25
00:01:58,676 --> 00:02:00,551
Subedi does not point it out.

26
00:02:00,551 --> 00:02:04,350
He puts the two things in one sentence and lets you do the arithmetic.

27
00:02:09,133 --> 00:02:17,325
Stanza one begins by saying it twice: I became soft, I became soft, as if discovering it while speaking.

28
00:02:17,325 --> 00:02:18,067
Why?

29
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:24,642
After the tumult he crashed on the eerie stillness, the frightening quiet after something terrible.

30
00:02:24,642 --> 00:02:37,846
He inherited the soft, handed down by the times, when the sky grew like crocuses over stones: crocuses are small spring flowers that push up through stony ground, and they will come back.

31
00:02:37,846 --> 00:02:43,132
Then the governing paradox: softness rose like a gale, tearing my roofs.

32
00:02:43,132 --> 00:02:45,176
Softness behaves like a storm.

33
00:02:45,176 --> 00:02:50,946
And the night speaks only the public language of posters and politics, rituals and reasons.

34
00:02:55,733 --> 00:02:57,219
Then four scenes.

35
00:02:57,219 --> 00:02:59,055
The hungry children in Thamel.

36
00:02:59,055 --> 00:03:08,560
A forlorn child with a transistor radio round his neck, running and wailing to find his mother in the corridors of violent history.

37
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:15,018
A man beaten before his family by nobody, for no reason, in no sensible times.

38
00:03:15,018 --> 00:03:16,437
And a blood stained shirt.

39
00:03:17,667 --> 00:03:18,971
Look closer at two.

40
00:03:18,971 --> 00:03:31,432
The radio is the child's only possession, maybe his living, and it is broadcasting the news of the violence that has lost him his mother: he carries the noise of history while running through it.

41
00:03:31,432 --> 00:03:34,205
And the beating: count the negatives.

42
00:03:34,205 --> 00:03:39,791
No reason, nobody, no reason again, no sensible times.

43
00:03:39,791 --> 00:03:41,484
Four in five lines.

44
00:03:41,484 --> 00:03:46,236
The repetition is the sound of a mind that cannot find an explanation.

45
00:03:46,236 --> 00:03:51,002
By nobody: in a collapsed time, violence stops having authors.

46
00:03:51,002 --> 00:03:52,213
It just happens.

47
00:03:53,533 --> 00:04:02,884
The shirt has no body; the person who wore it is never mentioned, which is how the dead of a conflict are often met, as a garment or a photograph.

48
00:04:02,884 --> 00:04:08,079
It can only whisper to the earth, because no human authority is listening.

49
00:04:08,079 --> 00:04:19,264
Then the poem's only complaint: you gave words but did not listen to them, gave storms but did not wait to see their Leela, the divine play, over the silent stone.

50
00:04:19,264 --> 00:04:20,240
Who is you?

51
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:23,443
A person, the country's leaders, or God.

52
00:04:23,443 --> 00:04:26,975
The poem does not say, and probably means all three.

53
00:04:31,767 --> 00:04:43,982
Stanza seven turns from witnessing to reflecting, and opens with the poem's one plainly hopeful image: crocuses have grown over the stone, I saw last moonlit night.

54
00:04:43,982 --> 00:04:48,813
The flowers really have come up through the rock, placed right after the worst stanzas.

55
00:04:48,813 --> 00:04:55,336
Then storms loiter in the narrow lanes, hanging about with no purpose, like the violence did.

56
00:04:55,336 --> 00:05:05,258
And the crescendo: the storm rages over silent pages, silent stones, silent shirts, and the silent ruins of the gods' temples.

57
00:05:05,258 --> 00:05:07,990
An enormous grief, and total silence.

58
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:17,587
Stanza eight: the mad time spools, winding up everything onto its reel, and everything is slightly wrong.

59
00:05:17,587 --> 00:05:19,527
Stone growing in flower.

60
00:05:19,527 --> 00:05:20,959
Deforested land.

61
00:05:20,959 --> 00:05:23,980
Birds on bizarre journeys over the warming earth.

62
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:33,485
And rhododendron, laligurans, Nepal's national flower, blooming in winter instead of spring: a symptom, not a pretty picture.

63
00:05:33,485 --> 00:05:38,993
Subedi puts environmental collapse beside political violence as one disorder.

64
00:05:38,993 --> 00:05:42,040
All in unison created this soft gale.

65
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:49,065
And the ending shrinks the public storm to something private: my soft storm dances in ripples of your uneasy lake.

66
00:05:53,833 --> 00:05:54,759
Themes.

67
00:05:54,759 --> 00:06:03,144
Softness as a response: when the times are cruel and nothing can be done, a decent person sees clearly and writes it down.

68
00:06:03,144 --> 00:06:07,572
Violence without meaning, by nobody for no reason.

69
00:06:07,572 --> 00:06:14,746
Silence and witness: everything the storm passes over is silent; only the poem speaks.

70
00:06:14,746 --> 00:06:23,723
And nature in disorder, the warming earth and winter rhododendron, beside a stubborn hope: crocuses over stone.

71
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:35,689
The form is free verse: nine stanzas, a hundred and six lines, no rhyme, no metre.

72
00:06:35,689 --> 00:06:43,541
What holds it together is anaphora: the phrase I became soft opens stanza after stanza, eight times.

73
00:06:43,541 --> 00:06:54,205
The oxymoron, soft storm, soft gale, is the poem's key figure: a contradiction that names a feeling both gentle and devastating.

74
00:06:54,205 --> 00:07:02,510
And notice the one rhyme that slips in, rages over silent pages, landing exactly on the idea of writing.

75
00:07:03,733 --> 00:07:05,206
Devices to quote.

76
00:07:05,206 --> 00:07:07,537
Oxymoron: soft storm.

77
00:07:07,537 --> 00:07:10,024
Anaphora: I became soft.

78
00:07:10,024 --> 00:07:14,099
The crocuses as a symbol of life in a hard place.

79
00:07:14,099 --> 00:07:17,953
Personification: the shirt whispering to the earth.

80
00:07:17,953 --> 00:07:21,508
Repetition: for no reason, for no reason.

81
00:07:21,508 --> 00:07:24,259
And the allusion to Leela, divine play.

82
00:07:26,667 --> 00:07:27,437
Pause and think.

83
00:07:34,733 --> 00:07:41,451
Two contradictory words joined: an oxymoron — a feeling both gentle and devastating.

84
00:07:43,433 --> 00:07:43,868
One more.

85
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:57,445
With the warming earth and deforested land, it joins environmental disorder to the political one.

86
00:07:59,633 --> 00:08:00,728
Quick recap.

87
00:08:00,728 --> 00:08:03,593
Subedi's Kathmandu in a violent decade.

88
00:08:03,593 --> 00:08:06,497
A softness inherited from the times.

89
00:08:06,497 --> 00:08:08,007
Four scenes of suffering.

90
00:08:08,007 --> 00:08:11,510
Crocuses over stone and a world out of season.

91
00:08:11,510 --> 00:08:15,040
And an oxymoron at the heart of it all: a soft storm.

92
00:08:19,233 --> 00:08:21,044
That completes the poems.

93
00:08:21,044 --> 00:08:24,274
Next we move to essays, starting with On Libraries.

94
00:08:24,274 --> 00:08:24,821
See you there.

